## 3. Project Tiering

In this architecture, cross-project references are constrained by a fixed tiering structure. This provides an acyclicity guarantee on dependencies. The purpose is to support cross-project collaboration and to ease sharing of common datasets. Work at every Tier is expected to provide contracted (production) outputs, which are made available as sources for subsequent Tiers.

```
Raw / Landing
  │
  └──► Tier 0 (Source Aggregates)           ◄─ may read Raw only              ┐
        │                                                                     ├─  Bronze Layer 
        ├──► Tier 0.5 (Core/ Consolidation) ◄─ may read Tier 0 (+ seeds)      ┘     (Single dbt project)
        │
        └──► Tier 1 (Domains)               ◄─ may read Tier 0 and Tier 0.5   ┐   Silver (internal/ intermediate) 
              │                                                               ├─    and Gold (exposure) Layer
              └──► Tier 2 (Cross-Domain)    ◄─ may read Tier 1 marts          ┘     (Multiple dbt projects)
```

**The entire Bronze Layer (Tier 0 and Tier 0.5) is managed as a single dbt project.** Within it, each source system is a *Source Aggregate module* (a folder), and Tier 0.5 is the *Core module*. The Bronze Layer produces all available sources for the Tier 1 projects. All analytics teams contribute to it as new analytic sources are required, and it is built out conceptually as a catalogue of available analytic sources.

Analytics projects will tend to focus on Tier 1. **Each domain-based Tier 1 project is a separate dbt project.** Each analytics project initially assesses the existing Bronze Layer contracted outputs as a base. Where the base is lacking, the analytics team contributes to Tier 0 or Tier 0.5. The Bronze Layer therefore provides shared analytics *internal* resources, receiving contributions from all analytics teams. Tier 1 and Tier 2 provide contracted outputs for exposure to the business.

NOTE:

> - Both dbt projects and the Snowflake platform capture lineage. Cross-Tier lineage (cross dbt project lineage) is captured within Snowflake itself.
> - Tier 0 is purely about data shaping and requires no business logic.
> - Tier 0.5 is a consolidation layer taking care of common cross-source identity and conformance.
> - Tiers 1 and 2 capture business logic. This is where data modelling comes to the fore. These Tiers expose the Gold layer dims, facts, aggs and semantic views.

### 3.1 Bronze Layer stewardship

Because every analytics team contributes to the Bronze Layer, its rules are only as strong as its review gate. The no-business-logic rule for Tier 0 is the load-bearing rule of this architecture, and it erodes one reasonable-looking pull request at a time unless it is actively defended.

- A named **Bronze steward team** owns the Bronze Layer repository, its CI, its release process and its run control.
- `CODEOWNERS` assigns each source module folder, and the Core module, to stewards. Contributions from any analytics team are welcome, but merges require steward approval.
- Stewards apply the structural join rule (4.4) and the scope-creep test (4.4.2) as review criteria.

### 3.2 Tier assignment

> 1. All Tier 0 and Tier 0.5 modules (the Bronze Layer) operate from a single "monorepo", using the standard dbt folder structure to separate source systems. The monorepo includes macros and tests. All outputs are contracted: column names, data types and constraints (although limited in Snowflake) are specified. Tests are performed. Documentation is pushed to Snowflake. All outputs are written to a single Bronze Snowflake database, with **one published schema per source module plus one for the Core module** (Section 10.3). The database is accessible only to analytics teams building out Tiers 1 and 2. A single dbt project and repo reinforces the perception of the Bronze Layer as an internal "catalogue" and starting point for Tier 1 analytics.
>
> 2. Sources with different load cadences do not require separate repos. Each source module is an independent publication unit with its own schedule (Sections 10 and 12).
>
> 3. If the monorepo is deemed too large, splits by source system are straightforward and yield essentially independent repos. This is because Tier 0 is purely about data shaping, with no cross-source joins. Splitting is not a necessity, and this document treats the Bronze Layer as a single dbt project and repo.
>
> 4. Tier 0 includes classical dbt staging, dbt snapshots and dbt seeds, as well as source system structural aggregates such as child field aggregations to parent records, or parent field attribution to child records. We borrow the term "aggregate" from Domain Driven Design, where such aggregates require no business logic. They are merely the rebuild of logical constructs from the source system's physical layer, which might be highly normalised. Where aggregation includes business logic, it must be implemented in Tier 1.
>
> 5. Tier 1 projects use dbt sources which are contracted production outputs of the Bronze Layer. This is a natural and very simple split of lineage, because Bronze outputs map to raw sources in a straightforward way. Tier 1 projects can immediately focus on intermediate and mart models: dims, facts, aggregates and semantic models. A Tier 1 project requiring specific data tests on its sources should contribute those tests to the Bronze Layer project.

