## 10. Write-Audit-Publish

### 10.1 Two requirements in tension

With a plain `dbt build`, tests run after models are replaced. By the time a test fails, published tables have already changed and downstream projects may already have been triggered. Tests observe the incident rather than prevent it.

Gating publication on tests fixes this. But a single gate over the whole Bronze Layer means one failing source blocks every analytics team. We need both:

- **(a)** Nothing ever builds on data that failed its tests.
- **(b)** A failure blocks only what depends on it.

### 10.2 Principle: failures block by staleness, not by status

A failed build never replaces published data. The last good version keeps serving, and the failure shows up downstream as **staleness**: the unit's watermark stops advancing.

Downstream units are never gated on an upstream *failure*. They are gated on upstream *freshness* against their own declared tolerance (Sections 11 and 12). This satisfies both requirements:

- Failed data is never published, so nothing can read it (a).
- Unrelated units are unaffected (b).
- Dependent units either proceed on last-good data within tolerance, or skip.

### 10.3 Publication units

A **publication unit** is the smallest set of objects published atomically. Each unit has exactly one consumer-visible (published) schema and one build schema, and optionally one internal schema.

| Tier | Unit | Example schemas |
|---|---|---|
| Tier 0 | One per source system | `bronze.erp`, `bronze.erp__build` |
| Tier 0.5 | One unit | `bronze.core`, `bronze.core__build` |
| Tier 1 / 2 | One per project | `finance.published`, `finance.published__build`, plus internal `finance.internal`, `finance.internal__build` |

Source systems work as units because Tier 0 forbids cross-source joins. Each source module is therefore an independent subgraph that can be published without reference to any other. Within a unit, publication is all-or-nothing, so consumers always see a consistent snapshot of a source. Across units, publication is independent, and each unit keeps its own schedule.

Each unit is deployed as **its own dbt project object** (from the same repo, for Bronze modules), because Snowflake does not support concurrent execution of a single project object (Section 2.1).

### 10.4 Publish procedure

One generic, caller's rights procedure, parameterised by unit and driven by run-control configuration (Section 12), performs:

```sql
-- 0. Gate: are upstream units within this unit's freshness tolerance,
--    and has anything upstream published since this unit last published?
--    If not, log SKIPPED and stop.

-- 1. Clone last good state (zero-copy; preserves incremental and snapshot state)
CREATE OR REPLACE SCHEMA bronze.erp__build CLONE bronze.erp;

-- 2. Build and test into the clone
EXECUTE DBT PROJECT bronze.ops.bronze_erp
  ARGS = 'build --select erp --vars {publish_unit: erp}';

-- 3. On success only: atomic publish, then restore container grants
ALTER SCHEMA bronze.erp__build SWAP WITH bronze.erp;
GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA bronze.erp TO DATABASE ROLE bronze_reader;

-- 4. Log PUBLISHED with watermark, or FAILED (Section 12)
```

If step 2 fails, nothing published has changed. There is no rollback, because there is nothing to roll back. The build schema is discarded and re-cloned at the next run's step 1.

For units with an internal schema, both schemas are cloned and built, then swapped **internal first, published second**. Only the published schema is consumer-visible, so atomicity is only required there.

**Schema redirection.** A custom `generate_schema_name` sends only the models of the unit being published (`var('publish_unit')`) into `__build` schemas. All other references resolve to published schemas. This is how the Core module reads last-good Tier 0 outputs through `ref()` while keeping in-project lineage. Sketch (to be validated, and extended for dev and CI targets):

```jinja
{% macro generate_schema_name(custom_schema_name, node) -%}
  {%- set base = (custom_schema_name or target.schema) | trim -%}
  {%- if target.name == 'prod' and base == var('publish_unit', none) -%}
    {{ base }}__build
  {%- else -%}
    {{ base }}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro %}
```

**Schema-relative references in views (subject to spike S1).** dbt renders fully qualified names, so a view in `bronze.erp__build` referencing a sibling table would still point at `bronze.erp__build` after the swap, reading the stale objects now living under that name. If Snowflake resolves an unqualified identifier inside a view relative to the view's own schema, then rendering **same-schema references from views** as bare identifiers makes views swap-safe. References to other schemas stay fully qualified; they follow the swap by name and are unaffected. Tables, incrementals and tests stay fully qualified, because their SQL resolves against the session, not against an object's own schema. Sketch:

```jinja
{% macro ref() %}
  {%- set rel = builtins.ref(*varargs, **kwargs) -%}
  {%- if model.config.materialized == 'view'
        and rel.database == this.database
        and rel.schema == this.schema -%}
    {%- do return(rel.include(database=false, schema=false)) -%}
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- do return(rel) -%}
{% endmacro %}
```

If spike S1 fails, views are not used for same-unit references inside published schemas, and that logic is made ephemeral instead.

### 10.5 Rules

1. **Anything published that reads raw data is materialised** (table, incremental or snapshot). A view over raw changes the moment data lands, so it cannot be gated. This rule holds regardless of the outcome of spike S1.
2. **Views in published schemas** may reference other units' objects (fully qualified), and same-unit objects only through bare identifiers once spike S1 confirms schema-relative resolution. Snowflake semantic views are included in this rule and in spike S1.
3. **Test severity carries meaning.** `error` blocks publication of the unit; `warn` publishes and is logged. Only tests that evidence wrong data are `error`: grain, FK relationships, row-count guards, contracts and crosswalk match-rate floors.
4. **Retries are idempotent.** Every run starts from a clone of the last good state, so a failed run can simply be re-run. This holds for incrementals and snapshots alike.
5. **Consumer roles are never granted on build schemas.** Grants on child objects travel with a schema clone; grants on the schema itself do not. The procedure therefore re-applies schema USAGE after every swap. Identical future grants are defined on both published and build schemas.
6. **One unit, one dbt project object.**

### 10.6 Failure behaviour

| Failure | Published result | Downstream |
|---|---|---|
| `erp` test fails | `bronze.erp` stays at last good | `crm` unaffected. `core` and ERP-dependent Tier 1 projects proceed on last-good ERP if within tolerance, otherwise skip. Their watermarks inherit ERP's older watermark |
| `core` crosswalk match rate below floor | Previous crosswalk keeps serving | All consumers stay on the last accepted identity mapping |
| `finance` build fails | Finance marts stay at last good | Tier 2 gated by finance freshness |
| Raw ERP batch does not land | No new landing-complete record; raw ERP watermark stops advancing | Staleness attributable to ingestion in run control (Section 12.4) |

### 10.7 Spikes required before the detailed design

| # | Spike | Question |
|---|---|---|
| S1 | View-relative resolution | Create view `s1.v` as `select * from t`, swap `s1` with `s2`, check which `t` the view reads. Also confirm `CREATE VIEW` accepts the bare identifier at creation, and repeat for semantic views |
| S2 | Swap and grants | Measure the privilege gap between `SWAP` and `GRANT USAGE`; confirm whether future grants move with the schema object |
| S3 | Failure signalling | Does `EXECUTE DBT PROJECT` raise, or return a status, on test failure inside a procedure? |
| S4 | Snapshots | Confirm snapshot correctness under clone-build-discard-retry |
| S5 | Clone cost | Clone time and storage behaviour for the largest incremental models |
| S6 | Macros across targets | `generate_schema_name` and `ref` overrides across dev, CI and prod |
| S7 | Dynamic tables (optional) | Whether Tier 0 aggregates as dynamic tables can coexist with Write-Audit-Publish and contracts |

